<h2>The architecture: excluded debts under Part III of the IBC</h2>
<p>Part III of the IBC governs insolvency and bankruptcy of individuals and partnership firms. Its machinery — the fresh start process for the smallest debtors, the insolvency resolution process built around a repayment plan, and bankruptcy with eventual discharge — all works on the debtor's <em>qualifying</em> debts. Section 79(15) then carves out the debts that the process cannot reach. Excluded debts include:</p>
<div class="check">
  <ul>
    <li>liability to pay fines imposed by a court or tribunal;</li>
    <li>liability to pay damages for negligence, nuisance or breach of a statutory, contractual or other legal obligation;</li>
    <li><strong>liability to pay maintenance to any person under any law for the time being in force;</strong></li>
    <li>liability in relation to a student loan; and</li>
    <li>any other debt prescribed.</li>
  </ul>
</div>
<p>The phrase "under any law" matters. It covers maintenance under Section 144 of the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023 (formerly Section 125 CrPC), interim and permanent alimony under Sections 24–25 of the Hindu Marriage Act, 1955, monetary relief and maintenance under the Protection of Women from Domestic Violence Act, 2005, maintenance under the Hindu Adoptions and Maintenance Act, 1956, parallel provisions of other personal laws, and orders for parents under the Maintenance and Welfare of Parents and Senior Citizens Act, 2007.</p>

<h2>What exclusion means in each process</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Process</th><th>Effect on ordinary debts</th><th>Effect on maintenance</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Fresh start (Chapter II, Part III)</td><td>Qualifying small debts can be written off</td><td>Maintenance is not a qualifying debt; it survives in full</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Insolvency resolution process (Chapter III)</td><td>Debts restructured under a repayment plan approved by creditors</td><td>Maintenance stays outside the plan and continues to accrue and bind</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Bankruptcy (Chapter IV)</td><td>Discharge releases the bankrupt from bankruptcy debts</td><td>Discharge does not release excluded debts; arrears and future maintenance remain enforceable</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Corporate insolvency (Part II)</td><td>Moratorium protects the company; resolution plan binds its creditors</td><td>Personal maintenance liability of a promoter or director is untouched by the company's CIRP</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Why Parliament drew the line here</h2>
<p>Insolvency law balances two interests: giving an honest debtor a second chance, and paying creditors fairly from limited assets. Maintenance belongs to neither side of that commercial ledger. It is not a bargain gone wrong but a status obligation — the law's insistence that dependants not be left destitute. Treating it as just another unsecured claim would let the insolvency process do what the maintenance jurisdiction exists to prevent. The exclusion keeps the two regimes in their lanes: creditors share the estate; dependants keep their statutory lifeline.</p>

<h2>Practical pointers for the maintenance holder</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Do not stop execution</strong> — if an insolvency filing is brandished, continue recovery proceedings and place Section 79(15) on record where any stay is claimed.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Scrutinise the plea of poverty</strong> — insolvency filings, like business losses, are a change-of-circumstance argument at best; the defaulter must prove real inability, and courts assess earning capacity, not just declared income.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Watch the assets</strong> — where personal assets are being moved into corporate structures or family names ahead of claims, seek interim protection; transfers designed to defeat maintenance invite close scrutiny.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Use the full toolkit</strong> — warrants for arrears, attachment of salary and property, and striking of defence for persistent interim default remain available regardless of insolvency rhetoric.</div>
</div>

<h2>And for the paying side</h2>
<p>A payer in genuine financial collapse is not without remedies — but the remedy lies in the maintenance jurisdiction, not the insolvency forum. The honest course is an application to vary the order on proof of changed circumstances, with full disclosure of the insolvency proceedings, assets and current income. What the law does not permit is using the IBC as a shield: the excluded-debt provision was written precisely to stop that argument before it starts.</p>
<div class="note">
  <p>This article describes the interaction of two statutory regimes in general terms and is not legal advice. Specific cases turn on their facts and the orders actually in force.</p>
</div>
